.TH "BEAR" "1" "July 3, 2026" "Bear User Manuals"
.SH NAME
Bear \- a tool to generate compilation database for Clang tooling.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]bear\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] [\-\-] [\f[I]BUILD_COMMAND\f[R]\&...]
.PP
\f[B]bear intercept\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] [\-\-]
\f[I]BUILD_COMMAND\f[R]\&...
.PP
\f[B]bear semantic\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bear is a tool that generates a JSON compilation database for Clang
tooling by intercepting command executions during the build process.
The JSON compilation database is used in the Clang project to provide
information about how individual compilation units were processed,
enabling tools like clang\-tidy, clangd, and other Clang\-based analysis
tools to understand your project\(cqs build configuration.
.PP
Bear operates by intercepting system calls during the build process to
capture compilation commands.
It supports two main interception methods: dynamic library preloading
(on Unix\-like systems) and wrapper executables (cross\-platform).
The captured commands are then filtered through semantic analysis to
identify actual compiler invocations and generate the final compilation
database.
.PP
Bear can operate in three modes:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Combined mode\f[R] (default): Runs both interception and semantic
analysis in sequence
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Intercept mode\f[R]: Only captures build events to an intermediate
file
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Semantic mode\f[R]: Processes previously captured events to
generate the compilation database
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-c, \-\-config\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Specify a configuration file path.
The configuration file controls output formatting, compiler recognition,
source filtering, and duplicate handling.
.TP
\f[B]\-o, \-\-output\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Specify the output file path (default:
\f[CR]compile_commands.json\f[R]).
The output is a JSON compilation database.
.TP
\f[B]\-a, \-\-append\f[R]
Append results to an existing output file instead of overwriting it.
This allows incremental updates to the compilation database.
New entries are placed before the existing ones, so when a source file
is rebuilt its newest invocation survives duplicate filtering and
replaces the stale entry (see the \f[CR]duplicates\f[R] section).
.TP
\f[B]\-h, \-\-help\f[R]
Print help information.
.TP
\f[B]\-V, \-\-version\f[R]
Print version information.
.SH COMMANDS
Calling bear without commands will execute the combined mode, and will
intercept the compiler calls and generate a compilation database as
output.
.SS bear intercept
Intercepts command execution events during the build process and saves
them to an events file for later processing.
.PP
\f[B]bear intercept\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] [\-\-]
\f[I]BUILD_COMMAND\f[R]\&...
.SS bear semantic
Processes previously captured events to generate a compilation database
through semantic analysis.
.PP
\f[B]bear semantic\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.SH OUTPUT
Bear generates a JSON compilation database conforming to the \c
.UR https://clang.llvm.org/docs/JSONCompilationDatabase.html
Clang JSON Compilation Database
.UE \c
\ specification.
The output is a JSON array of compilation entry objects.
.SS Entry Format
Each compilation database entry contains the following fields:
.TP
\f[B]directory\f[R]
The working directory of the compilation (absolute path)
.TP
\f[B]file\f[R]
The main translation unit source file (absolute path)
.TP
\f[B]arguments\f[R]
The compilation command as an array of strings (preferred format)
.TP
\f[B]command\f[R]
The compilation command as a single shell\-escaped string (alternative
to arguments)
.TP
\f[B]output\f[R]
The output file produced by compilation (optional, absolute path)
.SS Output Formatting
The output format can be controlled through the configuration file:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Path resolution\f[R]: Paths can be formatted as absolute, relative,
canonical, or as\-is
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Entry format\f[R]: Choose between arguments array (preferred) or
command string
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Field inclusion\f[R]: Control whether the output field is included
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source filtering\f[R]: Include/exclude files based on directory
rules
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Duplicate filtering\f[R]: Remove duplicate entries based on
configurable field matching
.PP
Bear generates entries where all paths are absolute by default, and uses
the \f[CR]arguments\f[R] field instead of \f[CR]command\f[R] to avoid
shell escaping issues.
.SH CONFIG FILE
Bear uses a YAML configuration file to control its behavior.
The configuration file follows a structured schema with several main
sections.
.SS Configuration Schema
.IP
.EX
schema\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq4.1\(dq
intercept\f[B]:\f[R]
  mode\f[B]:\f[R] wrapper
compilers\f[B]:\f[R]
  \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /usr/bin/cc
    as\f[B]:\f[R] gcc
  \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /usr/local/bin/gcc
    ignore\f[B]:\f[R] true
sources\f[B]:\f[R]
  directories\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /project/tests
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
  files\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dqmoc_*.cpp\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq*.pb.cc\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
duplicates\f[B]:\f[R]
  match_on\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] file
    \f[B]\-\f[R] arguments
format\f[B]:\f[R]
  paths\f[B]:\f[R]
    directory\f[B]:\f[R] canonical
    file\f[B]:\f[R] canonical
  entries\f[B]:\f[R]
    use_array_format\f[B]:\f[R] true
    include_output_field\f[B]:\f[R] true
  arguments\f[B]:\f[R]
    from_response_files\f[B]:\f[R] false
    from_environment\f[B]:\f[R] true
.EE
.PP
This example configuration file: sets the interception mode to
\f[CR]wrapper\f[R], hints the \f[CR]/usr/bin/cc\f[R] to be the main
compiler in this project, which is the GNU compiler, hints to ignore the
\f[CR]/usr/local/bin/gcc\f[R] compilers from the project, instructs to
ignore files from \f[CR]/project/tests\f[R], instructs to drop generated
Qt moc output (\f[CR]moc_*.cpp\f[R]) and protobuf stubs
(\f[CR]*.pb.cc\f[R]) by filename, instructs to detect duplicates based
on the \f[CR]file\f[R] and \f[CR]arguments\f[R] fields of the output
file, instructs to format the output to use canonical path for the
\f[CR]file\f[R] and \f[CR]directory\f[R] fields of the output file,
instructs to use the \f[CR]arguments\f[R] over the \f[CR]command\f[R]
field in the output file, instructs to include the \f[CR]output\f[R]
field in the output file.
.SS Configuration Sections
The configuration file uses schema version \f[CR]4.1\f[R] and has the
following structure:
.SS intercept
Controls the command interception method:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mode\f[R]: \f[CR]preload\f[R] (Unix) or \f[CR]wrapper\f[R]
(cross\-platform)
.SS compilers
Contains hints about what compiler needs to be recognized and what that
compiler is.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]path\f[R]: Path to the compiler executable
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]as\f[R]: Compiler type hint for semantic analysis.
Valid values are: \f[CR]gcc\f[R], \f[CR]clang\f[R], \f[CR]flang\f[R],
\f[CR]intel\-fortran\f[R], \f[CR]cray\-fortran\f[R], \f[CR]cuda\f[R],
\f[CR]msvc\f[R], \f[CR]clang\-cl\f[R], \f[CR]intel_cc\f[R],
\f[CR]nvidia\-hpc\f[R], \f[CR]armclang\f[R], \f[CR]ibm_xl\f[R],
\f[CR]vala\f[R], \f[CR]mpi\f[R], \f[CR]cray\-cc\f[R], \f[CR]qnx\f[R],
\f[CR]nasm\f[R], \f[CR]fasm\f[R], \f[CR]swift\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ignore\f[R]: Whether to ignore this compiler.
.PP
The generic compiler names \f[CR]cc\f[R], \f[CR]c++\f[R], and the HPE
Cray PrgEnv wrapper \f[CR]CC\f[R] default to GCC/Clang semantics chosen
by probing the executable\(cqs \f[CR]\-\-version\f[R] output (since the
same basename can be a different compiler depending on the platform or,
for \f[CR]CC\f[R], the loaded Cray programming environment).
On platforms where the probe cannot classify the executable, use the
\f[CR]as\f[R] field to override:
.IP
.EX
compilers\f[B]:\f[R]
  \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /usr/bin/cc
    as\f[B]:\f[R] clang
  \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /usr/bin/c++
    as\f[B]:\f[R] clang
.EE
.PP
MPI compiler wrappers (Open MPI/MPICH\(cqs \f[CR]mpicc\f[R],
\f[CR]mpicxx\f[R], \f[CR]mpic++\f[R], \f[CR]mpiCC\f[R],
\f[CR]mpifort\f[R], \f[CR]mpif77\f[R], \f[CR]mpif90\f[R]) are recognized
automatically, without any configuration.
The wrapper is recorded as the compiler exactly as invoked \(en Bear
does not expand it to the underlying compiler command it wraps.
Clang tooling that needs the wrapper\(cqs baked\-in include paths can
point at the wrapper directly (e.g.\ clangd\(cqs
\f[CR]\-\-query\-driver\f[R]).
Intel MPI\(cqs wrappers (\f[CR]mpiicc\f[R], \f[CR]mpiicpc\f[R],
\f[CR]mpiicx\f[R], \f[CR]mpiicpx\f[R], \f[CR]mpiifort\f[R],
\f[CR]mpiifx\f[R]) are recognized as the Intel compilers they front,
using Intel\(cqs flag semantics.
The launchers \f[CR]mpirun\f[R] and \f[CR]mpiexec\f[R] are not
recognized: they execute programs, they do not compile.
.PP
The Cray Compiling Environment (CCE) C/C++ compiler names
\f[CR]craycc\f[R], \f[CR]crayCC\f[R], and \f[CR]craycxx\f[R] are
recognized automatically, using Clang flag semantics (CCE C/C++ is
Clang\-based).
The HPE Cray PrgEnv wrapper \f[CR]CC\f[R] is classified by the same
version probe as \f[CR]cc\f[R]/\f[CR]c++\f[R]: it resolves to CCE Clang
under PrgEnv\-cray, GCC under PrgEnv\-gnu, and so on, matching whatever
compiler module is currently loaded.
A programming environment whose compiler prints a version banner the
probe does not recognize (for example \f[CR]nvc++\f[R] under
PrgEnv\-nvidia) is not classified; use the \f[CR]as\f[R] field on that
path to override.
.PP
AMD\(cqs ROCm compiler names \f[CR]amdclang\f[R], \f[CR]amdclang++\f[R],
and \f[CR]hipcc\f[R] are recognized automatically, using Clang flag
semantics; \f[CR]amdflang\f[R] is recognized automatically, using Flang
flag semantics.
\f[CR]hipcc\f[R] is a compiler driver (it calls clang or nvcc depending
on target), the same way \f[CR]nvcc\f[R] is; Bear records the driver
invocation as executed.
AOCC\(cqs plain \f[CR]clang\f[R]/\f[CR]clang++\f[R]/\f[CR]flang\f[R]
names were already covered by the existing Clang/Flang recognition.
.PP
QNX\(cqs compiler driver names \f[CR]qcc\f[R] and \f[CR]q++\f[R] are
recognized automatically, using GCC flag semantics (QNX\(cqs toolchain
is GCC\-backed).
QNX\(cqs variant selector, \f[CR]\-V\f[R]
(e.g.\ \f[CR]\-Vgcc_ntoaarch64le\f[R], or bare \f[CR]\-V\f[R] to list
available variants), is always treated as a driver option and is never
mistaken for a source file.
.PP
Emscripten\(cqs driver names \f[CR]emcc\f[R] and \f[CR]em++\f[R]
(including the \f[CR]emcc.py\f[R]/\f[CR]em++.py\f[R] spellings) and
Texas Instruments\(cq \f[CR]tiarmclang\f[R] are recognized
automatically, using Clang flag semantics.
In preload mode the underlying \f[CR]clang\f[R] child process that
\f[CR]emcc\f[R]/\f[CR]em++\f[R] spawn may be intercepted too; the
default duplicate detection collapses the pair to a single entry
recording the driver invocation.
Microchip\(cqs XC8 driver names \f[CR]xc8\-cc\f[R] and \f[CR]xc8\f[R]
are recognized automatically, using GCC flag semantics; the
\f[CR]xc16\-gcc\f[R] and \f[CR]xc32\-gcc\f[R] names were already covered
by the existing cross\-compiler prefix recognition.
.PP
Compiler launchers (\f[CR]ccache\f[R], \f[CR]distcc\f[R],
\f[CR]sccache\f[R], \f[CR]icecc\f[R]) that carry the real compiler in
their arguments are recorded as the real compiler\(cqs compilation:
\f[CR]ccache gcc \-c main.c\f[R] produces an entry for
\f[CR]gcc \-c main.c\f[R], with the launcher token dropped.
A launcher invocation that does not name a recognized compiler, or a
launcher wrapping another launcher, produces no entry.
icecream\(cqs \f[CR]icerun\f[R] is not a compiler launcher (it runs
arbitrary commands on the cluster) and is not recognized.
.PP
The standalone assemblers \f[CR]nasm\f[R], \f[CR]yasm\f[R], and
\f[CR]fasm\f[R] are recognized automatically, so assembly language
servers (for example asm\-lsp) can read per\-file assembler flags from
\f[CR]compile_commands.json\f[R].
Assembly compiled through a C/C++ compiler driver (for example
\f[CR]gcc \-c foo.s\f[R]) was already recorded via that driver\(cqs own
entry before this support existed; both paths now produce entries.
The GNU assembler \f[CR]as\f[R] is deliberately not recognized: gcc and
clang spawn it internally on a temporary \f[CR].s\f[R] file for every
ordinary C compile, and recognizing it would pollute the database with
one throwaway entry per compilation, using a temporary filename that
duplicate detection cannot collapse.
MASM (\f[CR]ml\f[R], \f[CR]ml64\f[R]) is out of scope (Windows\-only, no
recorded demand).
.PP
The Swift compiler \f[CR]swiftc\f[R] is recognized automatically; see
\(lqSwift Projects\(rq below for its whole\-module entry shape.
The \f[CR]swift\f[R] subcommand driver (\f[CR]swift build\f[R],
\f[CR]swift run\f[R], \f[CR]swift package\f[R]) is not recognized \(en
it is a subcommand dispatcher, not a compiler invocation.
.SS sources
Filtering functionality based on the source file location and filename.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]directories\f[R]: List of directory\-based inclusion/exclusion
rules
.PP
Directory rules are evaluated in order, with the last matching rule
determining inclusion/exclusion.
Empty directories list means include everything.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]files\f[R]: List of filename\-glob inclusion/exclusion rules, each
with a \f[B]pattern\f[R] and an \f[B]action\f[R] (\f[CR]include\f[R] or
\f[CR]exclude\f[R])
.PP
Filename\-pattern rules exist to drop machine\-generated sources \(en Qt
\f[CR]moc\f[R] output, protobuf stubs, and the output of other code
generators \(en from the compilation database, so that linters and
editors act only on hand\-written code.
This is off by default: with no \f[CR]files\f[R] rules configured, the
output is unchanged.
A pattern with no path separator (\f[CR]/\f[R], or \f[CR]\(rs\f[R] on
Windows) matches the source file\(cqs basename; a pattern containing a
separator matches the full source path as it appears in the entry, so
patterns should use the same path format as configured in
\f[CR]format.paths.file\f[R].
File\-pattern rules are evaluated in order, with the last matching rule
determining inclusion/exclusion, the same precedence the directory rules
use; a file matched by no pattern rule is included.
Directory rules and file\-pattern rules compose: an entry is emitted
only when both accept it.
An invalid pattern is rejected during configuration validation with an
error identifying the offending rule.
.PP
A common recipe for a Qt/protobuf project:
.IP
.EX
sources\f[B]:\f[R]
  files\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dqmoc_*.cpp\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq*.pb.cc\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq*.pb.h\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
.EE
.SS duplicates
Filtering functionality based on duplicate detection.
Here you can define which fields of the output file should be used in
the duplicate detection.
Two entries are duplicates when all of the configured fields match; the
first occurrence is kept and later duplicates are dropped.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]match_on\f[R]: List of fields to use for duplicate detection (file,
arguments, directory, command, output).
The default is \f[CR]directory\f[R] and \f[CR]file\f[R], so by default
one entry is kept per source file per directory, regardless of the
compiler arguments.
A consequence is that when a single build compiles the same file from
the same directory with different flags, only one entry is kept (the
first seen); add \f[CR]arguments\f[R] to \f[CR]match_on\f[R] to keep a
separate entry per configuration.
Combined with \f[CR]\-\-append\f[R] (new entries first), the default
means a rebuilt file\(cqs newest invocation replaces its stale entry.
.SS format
Output formatting configuration:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]paths.directory\f[R] and \f[B]paths.file\f[R]: How to format paths
of these fields.
The allowed values are:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]as\-is\f[R]: No transformation,
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]canonical\f[R]: Resolve to canonical path,
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]relative\f[R]: Make relative to directory field,
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]absolute\f[R]: Convert to absolute path,
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]entries.use_array_format\f[R]: Use the arguments array (default)
instead of the command string.
Set to \f[CR]false\f[R] for consumers that read only the
\f[CR]command\f[R] field.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]entries.include_output_field\f[R]: Include output field in entries
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]arguments.from_response_files\f[R]: Replace \f[CR]\(atfile\f[R]
response\-file references in each entry\(cqs arguments with the
file\(cqs tokenized contents (resolved relative to the compiler\(cqs
working directory, expanded recursively, MSVC/clang\-cl using Windows
quoting and other compilers using GCC/Clang quoting).
Disabled by default, in which case an \f[CR]\(atfile\f[R] argument is
recorded verbatim.
Missing or unreadable files are left literal with a warning.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]arguments.from_environment\f[R]: Fold compiler environment
variables that act as implicit flags into each entry\(cqs arguments \(en
GCC/Clang header\-search paths (\f[CR]CPATH\f[R],
\f[CR]C_INCLUDE_PATH\f[R], \f[CR]CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH\f[R],
\f[CR]OBJC_INCLUDE_PATH\f[R]) become include flags, and MSVC\(cqs
\f[CR]CL\f[R] / \f[CR]_CL_\f[R] become leading / trailing options.
Enabled by default.
Set to \f[CR]false\f[R] to record only the flags that appeared on the
command line.
(This is unrelated to the \f[CR]CC=\(dqgcc \-std=c11\(dq\f[R] convention
handled during interception.)
.SS Default Configuration
If no configuration file is specified, Bear uses built\-in defaults
optimized for most use cases.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]RUST_LOG\f[R]
Controls the logging level for Bear\(cqs internal operations.
This environment variable is essential for troubleshooting and debugging
Bear\(cqs behavior.
.RS
.PP
Supported log levels (in order of verbosity):
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]error\f[R] \- Only show critical errors
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]warn\f[R] \- Show warnings and errors
.PD 0
.P
.PD
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]info\f[R] \- Show informational messages, warnings, and errors
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]debug\f[R] \- Show detailed debugging information
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.EX
RUST_LOG=debug bear \-\- make all
RUST_LOG=info bear intercept \-\- cmake \-\-build .
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
The configuration file \f[CR]bear.yml\f[R] is searched in the following
locations, in order:
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]./bear.yml\f[B]\f[R]
The current working directory.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Bear/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Unix)
When \f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is set.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]$HOME/.config/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]$HOME/.config/Bear/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Unix)
When \f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is unset.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]%LOCALAPPDATA%\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]%LOCALAPPDATA%\(rsBear\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Windows)
When \f[CR]%LOCALAPPDATA%\f[R] is set.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]%APPDATA%\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]%APPDATA%\(rsBear\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Windows)
When \f[CR]%APPDATA%\f[R] is set.
.PP
The first file found is loaded; remaining locations are not consulted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Bear returns the exit status of the executed build command when running
in combined or intercept mode.
When the build command succeeds, Bear returns 0.
When the build command fails, Bear returns the same non\-zero exit code.
.PP
In semantic mode, Bear returns 0 on success and a non\-zero exit code if
semantic analysis fails.
.PP
If Bear itself encounters an internal error or crashes, it returns a
non\-zero exit code regardless of the build command\(cqs status.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
The potential problems you can face with are: the build with and without
Bear behaves differently or the output is empty.
.SS Debug Logging
\f[B]Before reporting any issues\f[R], always run Bear with debug
logging enabled:
.IP
.EX
RUST_LOG=debug bear \-\- your\-build\-command
.EE
.PP
This will provide detailed information about Bear\(cqs internal
operations.
And the debug output is essential for diagnosing problems and \f[B]must
be included\f[R] in any bug reports.
.SS Common Issues
The most common cause for empty outputs is that the build command did
not execute any commands.
The reason for that could be, because incremental builds not running the
compilers if everything is up\-to\-date.
Remember, Bear does not understand the build file (eg.: makefile), but
intercepts the executed commands.
.PP
The other common cause for empty output is that the build has a
\(lqconfigure\(rq step, which captures the compiler to build the
project.
In case of Bear is using the \f[I]wrapper\f[R] mode, it needs to run the
configure step with Bear too (and discard that output), before run the
build with Bear.
.SS GLIBC Version Errors in Cross\-Compilation
When the build runs compilers from a cross\-compilation SDK and you see
an error like
\f[CR]version \(aqGLIBC_2.33\(aq not found (required by .../libexec.so)\f[R],
Bear\(cqs preload library was built against a newer glibc than the SDK
toolchain provides.
The library must be ABI\-compatible not only with the host system but
also with the libc the intercepted compiler process loads from the SDK
sysroot.
Build (or obtain) a Bear linked against a glibc no newer than the
SDK\(cqs.
See the project wiki Troubleshooting page (LD_PRELOAD errors) for
diagnostic commands.
.SS Compiler Env Vars With Flags
In wrapper mode, Bear accepts compiler environment variables that carry
a trailing flag or two, matching the GNU Make convention:
.IP
.EX
CC=\(dqgcc \-std=c11\(dq make
CXX=\(dqclang++ \-stdlib=libc++\(dq make
CC=\(dq/usr/local/bin/gcc \-m32\(dq make
.EE
.PP
Bear splits the value on whitespace, resolves the first token as the
compiler, and rewrites the variable so the build still sees the flags
(\f[CR]CC=<wrapper_path> \-std=c11\f[R]).
.PP
This convention is a Unix / GNU Make inheritance; it applies when
\f[CR]bear \-\- make\f[R] runs under sh/bash (including MSYS2, Git Bash,
WSL on Windows), not under native Windows build systems (MSBuild,
\f[CR]nmake\f[R], \f[CR]cmd\f[R], PowerShell), which do not consume
\f[CR]CC\f[R]/\f[CR]CXX\f[R] from the environment.
.PP
For anything beyond simple whitespace\-separated tokens (flags
containing spaces, shell quoting, metacharacters, command
substitutions), use \f[CR]CFLAGS\f[R], \f[CR]CXXFLAGS\f[R], or
\f[CR]LDFLAGS\f[R] instead of packing it into \f[CR]CC\f[R].
Those variables are the portable channel for compilation flags and every
build system expects them.
Bear does not parse or rewrite them; they reach the compiler untouched.
.SS Vala Projects
Bear records \f[CR]valac\f[R] invocations, producing one entry per
\f[CR]valac\f[R] invocation (valac compiles all of a target\(cqs
\f[CR].vala\f[R]/\f[CR].gs\f[R] sources together as one translation
unit, so the entry\(cqs \f[CR]file\f[R] is the first source and every
source is kept in the command).
Two things are worth knowing:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]vala\-language\-server might require the command\-string form.\f[R]
It reads the \f[CR]command\f[R] field and ignores the
\f[CR]arguments\f[R] array, so build the database with the array format
disabled:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
format\f[B]:\f[R]
  entries\f[B]:\f[R]
    use_array_format\f[B]:\f[R] false
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Mixed C and Vala projects.\f[R] \f[CR]valac\f[R] transpiles to C
and then invokes a C compiler on the generated C, so the database also
contains entries for that generated C. A C language server such as
clangd indexes every entry and will emit unknown\-argument noise on the
\f[CR]valac\f[R] entries.
Exclude the Vala sources on the clangd side with a \f[CR].clangd\f[R]
file:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
If\f[B]:\f[R]
  PathMatch\f[B]:\f[R] .*\(rs.vala
Diagnostics\f[B]:\f[R]
  Suppress\f[B]:\f[R] \(aq*\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SS Swift Projects
Bear records \f[CR]swiftc\f[R] invocations.
Unlike \f[CR]valac\f[R], a whole\-module \f[CR]swiftc\f[R] invocation
that names several \f[CR].swift\f[R] sources produces one entry PER
source, not one combined entry \(en and every one of those entries
carries the COMPLETE invocation\(cqs arguments (every source in the
module, not just its own).
This matches the shape CMake\(cqs own Swift support emits and that
SourceKit\-LSP already consumes: per\-file tooling looks up a compile
command by file path, and whole\-module compilation means each file\(cqs
semantics genuinely depend on every other source in the invocation, so
no entry can be reduced to \(lqthis file only\(rq.
A larger whole\-module invocation therefore produces a database with
more duplicated argument data than a comparable GCC/Clang build; this is
expected, not a bug.
.PP
The internal per\-file \f[CR]swift\-frontend\f[R] jobs that
\f[CR]swiftc\f[R] spawns (and a legacy toolchain\(cqs
\f[CR]swiftc \-frontend\f[R] self\-invocation) are filtered out
automatically and produce no entries \(en only the user\-facing
\f[CR]swiftc\f[R] driver invocation is recorded.
.PP
\f[CR]\-index\-store\-path\f[R] is never injected by Bear; if the build
already passes it, it is recorded like any other flag, which benefits
SourceKit\-LSP\(cqs cross\-file indexing, but Bear itself does not add
it.
.PP
On macOS, Xcode\(cqs \f[CR]swiftc\f[R] is Apple\-signed, so System
Integrity Protection blocks \f[CR]DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES\f[R]; Bear\(cqs
wrapper interception mode applies there, the same as for any other
Apple\-signed compiler.
